  ("How integrated pest management (IPM) shapes a general plan",
   "<p>Most reputable providers follow <strong>integrated pest management</strong>, the approach the <a href=\"https://www.epa.gov/safepestcontrol/integrated-pest-management-ipm-principles\" rel=\"noopener\">U.S. Environmental Protection Agency</a> describes as a combination of common-sense practices that rely on inspection, monitoring and prevention first, and pesticides only as needed. In a home, that means identifying the pest, setting an action threshold (a single spider in a basement may not need treatment; a roach in the kitchen does), fixing the moisture or gap that let it in, and choosing the lowest-risk product that will work.</p><p>You will see IPM in the details of the visit: monitoring glue boards in the garage, bait instead of spray for ants, and notes about leaking hose bibs or firewood stacked against the house. Ask any provider how they apply IPM before you sign a plan.</p>"),
